<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#chakravarti-sanskrit-term">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chakravarti</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cakravartin, Chakravartin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A chakravarti, one who moves the wheels, is the ideal universal ruler of the historiography and religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a concept that may have developed by the time of the early Maurya kings in the 4th to 3rd centuries BCE. Tradition distinguishes three kinds: the emperor of all four continents, the ruler of one, and the king of only part of one. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the chakravarti is the secular counterpart of a buddha, and in Buddhism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> alike he upholds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>. Rulers associated with the title include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a>. The idea later evolved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">devaraja</a>, the divinity of kings, adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> and the Thai monarchs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chakravarti-sanskrit-term.jpg" alt="Chakravarti" width="330" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neoclassicism Enthusiast · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Relief_of_Ashoka,_circa_1st_century_BC,_from_the_Amaravathi_village,_Guntur_district,_Andhra_Pradesh_(India),_in_the_Guimet_Museum_(Paris).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
